# Packaging the TypeScript service

`@athrio/loom-service-typescript` is the first loadable language service. A project installs it to give its TypeScript product an editor program of its own, held apart from the frame's. The package builds to one ES module, `dist/index.js`, whose default export is the service — the artifact the dynamic loader imports at runtime.

## The manifest {Tangle}[package.json]

The `exports` field names the package's surface, and it is a single entry: the default `.`, the built `dist/index.js` whose default export is the service the loader reads. The `files` field ships only `dist`, so the published package is that one built file and its source map, and `publishConfig` makes the scoped package public. The `repository` field points npm at this package's directory in the monorepo, which the provenance signature verifies against the workflow that publishes it — a publish through Trusted Publishing fails without it.

The package carries two dependencies, which the build leaves out of the bundle, so the store installs them beside the service. Prettier formats a TypeScript section for a project that has no Prettier of its own. `vscode-jsonrpc` speaks to a language server the service runs as a child process, such as TypeScript 7's. The build bundles `volar-service-typescript` and Volar's language layers into `dist/index.js`, and the `prepack` script runs it before each publish, so the package always ships a fresh bundle. The runtime bridge reads `effect`, the `@athrio/loom-lang-services` contract, and the editor's TypeScript from the host at load. Those three are neither bundled nor installed — the host lends them, so a second copy never shadows the `TypescriptSdk` the host provides. The `devDependencies` exist only to build and type-check the source; what the running service shares with the host is fixed by the runtime version `Runtime` declares, not by an npm range.

{{ version = ${version} }}
{{ effect = ${effect} }}

=>

{
  "name": "@athrio/loom-service-typescript",
  "version": "::[version]",
  "description": "The loadable TypeScript language service: the product's own editor program, held apart from the frame.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "git+https://github.com/athrio-com/loom.git",
    "directory": "packages/loom-service-typescript"
  },
  "type": "module",
  "main": "./dist/index.js",
  "exports": {
    ".": "./dist/index.js"
  },
  "files": [
    "dist"
  ],
  "publishConfig": {
    "access": "public"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "vite build",
    "watch": "vite build --watch",
    "prepack": "bun run build"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "prettier": "^3.8.4",
    "vscode-jsonrpc": "^8.2.1"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@athrio/loom-lang-services": "workspace:*",
    "@volar/language-core": "^2.4.28",
    "@volar/language-service": "^2.4.28",
    "@volar/typescript": "^2.4.28",
    "effect": "::[effect]",
    "typescript": "^6.0.3",
    "volar-service-typescript": "^0.0.71",
    "vscode-uri": "^3.1.0"
  }
}

## The TypeScript config {Tangle}[tsconfig.json]

The config extends the workspace base, which supplies the compiler options and the Node and Bun types, and typechecks `src` and `test`. The tests belong to the project, so the editor checks a test with the same options as the code it tests. It never ships.

=>

{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "out"
  },
  "include": ["src", "test"]
}

## The licence {Tangle}[LICENSE]

npm ships a package's `LICENSE` whatever the `files` field says, and until now this package shipped none — an installer had the SPDX expression in the manifest and no text to read. The file below is that text.

It carries the MIT licence in full. Loom was dual-licensed under MIT and Apache-2.0 for a while, inheriting a Rust convention rather than a decision; the Apache branch bought a patent grant nobody was obliged to take, and dropping it left one licence to read and one string to keep consistent.

It also says what is *not* here. Athrio sells Loom Design under a licence of its own, and a reader who finds a permissive text here should not conclude it reaches that product too.

=>

